          {
            "heading": "Part E: Nishka ki baat poori hui",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Nishka ki baat poori hui",
            "body": "Nishka ne notebook mein likha: \"Pool ke dabbe mein NaCl(aq) hai: cathode par H⁺ jeeta (Na⁺ nahi), to **H₂**, aur anode par (khaas coating wale electrodes ki madad se) Cl⁻ ne OH⁻ ko hara diya, to **Cl₂**. Aur ye Cl₂ paani mein ghul kar pool ke kitanu maarti hai.\" Mr. Sathaye ne sir hilaya: \"Haan. Aur ek baat: Cl₂ aur NaOH aapas mein bhi mil sakte hain: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl + H₂O (Chapter 1 ka household bleach). Par wo kahani Topic 5 ki hai.\" Nishka ne puchha: \"Aur agar dabbe ke plate taambe ke hote, to?\" Mr. Sathaye: \"Tab anode ghul jaata, aur chlorine nahi banti. Isliye plate **inert** ya khaas coating wali hi hoti hain.\"",
            "bodyHinglish": "Nishka ne notebook mein likha: \"Pool ke dabbe mein NaCl(aq) hai: cathode par H⁺ jeeta (Na⁺ nahi), to **H₂**, aur anode par (khaas coating wale electrodes ki madad se) Cl⁻ ne OH⁻ ko hara diya, to **Cl₂**. Aur ye Cl₂ paani mein ghul kar pool ke kitanu maarti hai.\" Mr. Sathaye ne sir hilaya: \"Haan. Aur ek baat: Cl₂ aur NaOH aapas mein bhi mil sakte hain: Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaOCl + H₂O (Chapter 1 ka household bleach). Par wo kahani Topic 5 ki hai.\" Nishka ne puchha: \"Aur agar dabbe ke plate taambe ke hote, to?\" Mr. Sathaye: \"Tab anode ghul jaata, aur chlorine nahi banti. Isliye plate **inert** ya khaas coating wali hi hoti hain.\""
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Solved examples (electron gin kar)",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Solved examples (electron gin kar)",
            "body": "Is chapter mein hum bijli ke charge ka hisaab nahi lagate (wo aage ki class mein): sirf **electrons gin kar**, jo mole se judte hain.\n\n### Example 1: CuSO₄ ghol, inert electrodes: taamba, O₂ aur acid\n\n**Sawaal:** Platinum electrodes ke saath CuSO₄ ghol mein se bijli guzri aur cathode par **0.2 mol Cu** jama hua. Kitne mole electrons lage? Anode par kitni O₂ bani (mol aur gram)? Aur kitna H₂SO₄ bana (gram)?\n\n**Hal:**\n- Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu: 1 mol Cu ke liye 2 mol electrons. Electrons = 0.2 × 2 = **0.4 mol**.\n- Anode: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻: 4 mol electrons par 1 mol O₂. 0.4 mol electrons par O₂ = 0.4 ÷ 4 = **0.1 mol**. Mass = 0.1 × 32 = **3.2 g**.\n- 2CuSO₄ + 2H₂O → 2Cu + 2H₂SO₄ + O₂: 2 mol Cu ke saath 2 mol H₂SO₄. 0.2 mol Cu, to H₂SO₄ = **0.2 mol**. H₂SO₄ = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g/mol, to 0.2 × 98 = **19.6 g**.\n- **Jaanch (mass conservation, 0.2 mol ke liye):** left: 0.2 mol CuSO₄ (0.2 × 159.5 = 31.9 g) + 0.2 mol H₂O (3.6 g) = 35.5 g. Right: Cu 12.7 g + H₂SO₄ 19.6 g + O₂ 3.2 g = 35.5 g ✅.\n\n### Example 2: Acidified paani: kitna paani, kitni gas\n\n**Sawaal:** Acidified paani ke electrolysis mein 90 g paani poori tarah toot gaya. H₂ aur O₂ ke mass, mole, aur electrons kitne? Volume ratio kya?\n\n**Hal:**\n- H₂O = 18 g/mol, to 90 ÷ 18 = **5 mol**.\n- 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂: 5 mol paani se 5 mol H₂ aur 2.5 mol O₂.\n- H₂ = 5 × 2 = **10 g**. O₂ = 2.5 × 32 = **80 g**.\n- **Jaanch:** 10 + 80 = 90 g ✅.\n- Electrons: har H₂ ke liye 2 electrons, 5 mol H₂ ke liye **10 mol electrons**. Anode se bhi 2.5 × 4 = 10 mol electrons ✅.\n- Volume ratio H₂ : O₂ = 5 : 2.5 = **2 : 1** ✅.\n\n### Example 3: Concentrated NaCl: NaOH kitna?\n\n**Sawaal:** Concentrated NaCl (graphite electrodes) ke electrolysis mein cathode par **0.5 mol H₂** bani. Kitni Cl₂ bani? Kitna NaOH bana (gram)? Kitne electrons lage?\n\n**Hal:**\n- 2NaCl + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + Cl₂ + H₂: 1 mol H₂ ke saath 1 mol Cl₂ aur 2 mol NaOH.\n- Cl₂ = **0.5 mol** = 0.5 × 71 = 35.5 g (**zehreeli, sirf padhne ke liye**).\n- NaOH = 2 × 0.5 = **1.0 mol** = 1.0 × 40 = **40 g**. (NaOH ek tez kshaar hai, sirf padhne ke liye.)\n- Electrons: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂: 0.5 × 2 = **1.0 mol**. Anode se bhi 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻: 0.5 × 2 = 1.0 mol ✅.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Is chapter mein hum bijli ke charge ka hisaab nahi lagate (wo aage ki class mein): sirf **electrons gin kar**, jo mole se judte hain.\n\n### Example 1: CuSO₄ ghol, inert electrodes: taamba, O₂ aur acid\n\n**Sawaal:** Platinum electrodes ke saath CuSO₄ ghol mein se bijli guzri aur cathode par **0.2 mol Cu** jama hua. Kitne mole electrons lage? Anode par kitni O₂ bani (mol aur gram)? Aur kitna H₂SO₄ bana (gram)?\n\n**Hal:**\n- Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu: 1 mol Cu ke liye 2 mol electrons. Electrons = 0.2 × 2 = **0.4 mol**.\n- Anode: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻: 4 mol electrons par 1 mol O₂. 0.4 mol electrons par O₂ = 0.4 ÷ 4 = **0.1 mol**. Mass = 0.1 × 32 = **3.2 g**.\n- 2CuSO₄ + 2H₂O → 2Cu + 2H₂SO₄ + O₂: 2 mol Cu ke saath 2 mol H₂SO₄. 0.2 mol Cu, to H₂SO₄ = **0.2 mol**. H₂SO₄ = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98 g/mol, to 0.2 × 98 = **19.6 g**.\n- **Jaanch (mass conservation, 0.2 mol ke liye):** left: 0.2 mol CuSO₄ (0.2 × 159.5 = 31.9 g) + 0.2 mol H₂O (3.6 g) = 35.5 g. Right: Cu 12.7 g + H₂SO₄ 19.6 g + O₂ 3.2 g = 35.5 g ✅.\n\n### Example 2: Acidified paani: kitna paani, kitni gas\n\n**Sawaal:** Acidified paani ke electrolysis mein 90 g paani poori tarah toot gaya. H₂ aur O₂ ke mass, mole, aur electrons kitne? Volume ratio kya?\n\n**Hal:**\n- H₂O = 18 g/mol, to 90 ÷ 18 = **5 mol**.\n- 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂: 5 mol paani se 5 mol H₂ aur 2.5 mol O₂.\n- H₂ = 5 × 2 = **10 g**. O₂ = 2.5 × 32 = **80 g**.\n- **Jaanch:** 10 + 80 = 90 g ✅.\n- Electrons: har H₂ ke liye 2 electrons, 5 mol H₂ ke liye **10 mol electrons**. Anode se bhi 2.5 × 4 = 10 mol electrons ✅.\n- Volume ratio H₂ : O₂ = 5 : 2.5 = **2 : 1** ✅.\n\n### Example 3: Concentrated NaCl: NaOH kitna?\n\n**Sawaal:** Concentrated NaCl (graphite electrodes) ke electrolysis mein cathode par **0.5 mol H₂** bani. Kitni Cl₂ bani? Kitna NaOH bana (gram)? Kitne electrons lage?\n\n**Hal:**\n- 2NaCl + 2H₂O → 2NaOH + Cl₂ + H₂: 1 mol H₂ ke saath 1 mol Cl₂ aur 2 mol NaOH.\n- Cl₂ = **0.5 mol** = 0.5 × 71 = 35.5 g (**zehreeli, sirf padhne ke liye**).\n- NaOH = 2 × 0.5 = **1.0 mol** = 1.0 × 40 = **40 g**. (NaOH ek tez kshaar hai, sirf padhne ke liye.)\n- Electrons: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂: 0.5 × 2 = **1.0 mol**. Anode se bhi 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻: 0.5 × 2 = 1.0 mol ✅."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti 1: \"Cathode par sodium banega, kyunki ghol mein Na⁺ hai.\"**\nMaano Nishka ke dost ne kaha ki NaCl ke ghol se sodium nikaalenge. Par ghol mein cathode par do pehelwan hain: Na⁺ aur H⁺. Order mein H⁺ upar hai, wahi jeetta hai, to cathode par **H₂** banta hai. Sodium sirf **pighle** NaCl se milta hai (Chapter 3), jahan paani nahi hota.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Inert aur copper electrode ka CuSO₄ mein ek hi result hai.\"**\nDo beaker rakhte hain, dono mein neela CuSO₄. Beaker (a) mein graphite, beaker (b) mein taamba. Thodi der baad (a) ka neela rang halka, anode par bulbule; (b) ka neela rang waisa ka waisa aur anode patla. Dono mein cathode par taamba jamta hai, magar sirf (a) mein O₂ aur acid banta hai.\n\n**Galti 3: \"SO₄²⁻ anode par Cl⁻ jaisa discharge hota hai.\"**\nDilute H₂SO₄ ka electrolysis karte ho to anode par SO₂ ya SO₃ nahi, **O₂** milti hai. SO₄²⁻ paani wale ghol mein discharge nahi hota; OH⁻ hota hai. Isiliye acid \"sirf ions dene wala\" hai.\n\n**Galti 4: \"Anode negative hota hai.\"**\nTopic 2 yaad karo: electrolytic cell mein anode **positive** terminal se juda hai, aur wahi par anions **electron dete** hain (oxidation). Cathode negative hai aur wahi par reduction hota hai. Agar tum anode ko negative maanoge, to Cl⁻ ko galat electrode ki taraf bhejoge. Sahi ye hai: Cl⁻ (negative ion) anode (positive electrode) ki taraf jata hai, kyunki viprit charge khinchte hain.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti 1: \"Cathode par sodium banega, kyunki ghol mein Na⁺ hai.\"**\nMaano Nishka ke dost ne kaha ki NaCl ke ghol se sodium nikaalenge. Par ghol mein cathode par do pehelwan hain: Na⁺ aur H⁺. Order mein H⁺ upar hai, wahi jeetta hai, to cathode par **H₂** banta hai. Sodium sirf **pighle** NaCl se milta hai (Chapter 3), jahan paani nahi hota.\n\n**Galti 2: \"Inert aur copper electrode ka CuSO₄ mein ek hi result hai.\"**\nDo beaker rakhte hain, dono mein neela CuSO₄. Beaker (a) mein graphite, beaker (b) mein taamba. Thodi der baad (a) ka neela rang halka, anode par bulbule; (b) ka neela rang waisa ka waisa aur anode patla. Dono mein cathode par taamba jamta hai, magar sirf (a) mein O₂ aur acid banta hai.\n\n**Galti 3: \"SO₄²⁻ anode par Cl⁻ jaisa discharge hota hai.\"**\nDilute H₂SO₄ ka electrolysis karte ho to anode par SO₂ ya SO₃ nahi, **O₂** milti hai. SO₄²⁻ paani wale ghol mein discharge nahi hota; OH⁻ hota hai. Isiliye acid \"sirf ions dene wala\" hai.\n\n**Galti 4: \"Anode negative hota hai.\"**\nTopic 2 yaad karo: electrolytic cell mein anode **positive** terminal se juda hai, aur wahi par anions **electron dete** hain (oxidation). Cathode negative hai aur wahi par reduction hota hai. Agar tum anode ko negative maanoge, to Cl⁻ ko galat electrode ki taraf bhejoge. Sahi ye hai: Cl⁻ (negative ion) anode (positive electrode) ki taraf jata hai, kyunki viprit charge khinchte hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- Aqueous ghol mein **do group ke ions**: electrolyte ke aur paani ke (H⁺, OH⁻).\n- **Cation order** (aasaan pehle): Ag⁺, Cu²⁺, H⁺, Pb²⁺, Sn²⁺, Ni²⁺, Fe²⁺, Zn²⁺, Al³⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Na⁺, K⁺ (reactivity series ulta). **Anion order**: I⁻, Br⁻, Cl⁻, OH⁻; NO₃⁻ aur SO₄²⁻ paani wale ghol se discharge nahi hote.\n- Teen baatein: **order**, **concentration**, **electrode ka kism** (inert ya active).\n- Acidified paani (Pt): H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1. Concentrated NaCl (graphite): H₂, Cl₂, NaOH. Dilute NaCl: H₂ aur O₂. CuSO₄ (inert): Cu aur O₂, ghol amlin. CuSO₄ (Cu electrodes): anode ghulta, ghol waisa ka waisa.\n- Mass ke sawaal: **electrons gino** (Cu²⁺ ke liye 2, O₂ ke liye 4), phir mole ratio.\n- Chlorine **zehreeli** hai; har electrolysis sirf padhne ke liye ya teacher demo.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- Aqueous ghol mein **do group ke ions**: electrolyte ke aur paani ke (H⁺, OH⁻).\n- **Cation order** (aasaan pehle): Ag⁺, Cu²⁺, H⁺, Pb²⁺, Sn²⁺, Ni²⁺, Fe²⁺, Zn²⁺, Al³⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Na⁺, K⁺ (reactivity series ulta). **Anion order**: I⁻, Br⁻, Cl⁻, OH⁻; NO₃⁻ aur SO₄²⁻ paani wale ghol se discharge nahi hote.\n- Teen baatein: **order**, **concentration**, **electrode ka kism** (inert ya active).\n- Acidified paani (Pt): H₂ : O₂ = 2 : 1. Concentrated NaCl (graphite): H₂, Cl₂, NaOH. Dilute NaCl: H₂ aur O₂. CuSO₄ (inert): Cu aur O₂, ghol amlin. CuSO₄ (Cu electrodes): anode ghulta, ghol waisa ka waisa.\n- Mass ke sawaal: **electrons gino** (Cu²⁺ ke liye 2, O₂ ke liye 4), phir mole ratio.\n- Chlorine **zehreeli** hai; har electrolysis sirf padhne ke liye ya teacher demo."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "Har sawaal mein electrolyte, uski concentration aur electrodes diye gaye hain. Saare electrolysis **sirf padhne ke liye ya teacher demo** hain.\n\n1. Dilute calcium nitrate, Ca(NO₃)₂, solution is electrolysed using inert platinum electrodes. Name the ions present, the product at each electrode, and write the half-equations. State the volume ratio of the two gases.\n2. Concentrated hydrochloric acid is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Name the ions present and the product at each electrode, with half-equations. (Chlorine is poisonous: described only.)\n3. Dilute silver nitrate solution is electrolysed using inert platinum electrodes. Give the product at each electrode, write the overall equation, and say how the solution changes. Then predict what changes if the platinum is replaced by silver electrodes.\n4. Concentrated potassium iodide solution is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Give the product at each electrode, the substance left in the solution, and the overall equation. If 76.2 g of iodine is formed, find the moles of electrons used, the mass of hydrogen and the mass of KOH formed. (I = 127.)\n5. Concentrated copper(II) chloride solution is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. If 3.81 g of copper is deposited, find the moles of electrons, and the mass of chlorine formed. Why is this a described-only demonstration?\n6. Dilute sulphuric acid is electrolysed with a copper anode and a platinum cathode. Give the product at each electrode in the first few minutes, and say what colour change is seen in the solution.\n7. Dilute sodium sulphate solution is electrolysed using inert platinum electrodes. Give the products. If 0.5 mol of electrons pass, find the mass of each gas, and say how the concentration of Na₂SO₄ changes.\n8. A dilute solution contains silver nitrate, copper(II) nitrate and zinc nitrate. It is electrolysed with platinum electrodes for a long time. It is known that Ag⁺ is discharged more easily than Cu²⁺, Cu²⁺ more easily than H⁺, and H⁺ more easily than Zn²⁺, and that NO₃⁻ is not discharged. Give the order of the products at the cathode, and the product at the anode.\n9. Concentrated sodium bromide solution is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Name the products at both electrodes and the substance left in solution. Which is discharged first from a concentrated mixture of NaBr and NaCl at the anode? (Bromine is poisonous: described only.)\n10. Explain why very dilute NaCl solution gives oxygen at the anode while concentrated NaCl gives chlorine. Also explain why sodium metal can never be obtained by electrolysing an aqueous NaCl solution, and name the method used in Chapter 3.\n11. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Concentrated NaCl solution gives sodium at the cathode. (b) The anode is the positive electrode in an electrolytic cell. (c) With copper electrodes in CuSO₄ solution the blue colour fades. (d) In dilute H₂SO₄ (platinum electrodes), SO₄²⁻ is discharged at the anode. (e) Electrons travel through the solution from the cathode to the anode.\n12. As a paper task, arrange these cations in the order of discharge, easiest first: Zn²⁺, Ag⁺, Na⁺, H⁺, Ni²⁺. Then say which of them give way to H⁺ in a dilute aqueous solution, and which one can never be obtained from any aqueous solution.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Ions: Ca²⁺, NO₃⁻ (Ca(NO₃)₂ se) aur H⁺, OH⁻ (paani se). Cathode par **H₂** (H⁺ Ca²⁺ se aasaan): 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂. Anode par **O₂** (NO₃⁻ discharge nahi hota): 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻. Volume ratio H₂ : O₂ = **2 : 1**. Overall 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂ (Ca(NO₃)₂ sirf ions deta hai).\n2. Ions: H⁺, Cl⁻ (HCl se) aur H⁺, OH⁻ (paani se). Cathode: **H₂**: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Anode: ghol **concentrated** hai aur Cl⁻ order mein OH⁻ se pehle, isliye **Cl₂**: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. Ghol dheere-dheere HCl se khaali hota jaata hai. Chlorine zehreeli hai, isliye sirf fume cupboard mein teacher demo.\n3. Cathode par **Ag**: Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag. Anode par **O₂**: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻ (NO₃⁻ discharge nahi hota). Overall: 4AgNO₃ + 2H₂O → 4Ag + 4HNO₃ + O₂. Ghol mein **HNO₃** banta hai, ghol **amlin** hota hai aur Ag⁺ kam hota hai. **Silver electrodes** ke saath: anode par silver ghulta hai (Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻), koi O₂ nahi; cathode par Ag jamta hai; ghol ka concentration waisa ka waisa. Silver nitrate dag chhodta hai aur nuksandeh hai: sirf teacher ke saath.\n4. Ions: K⁺, I⁻, H⁺, OH⁻. Cathode: **H₂** (K⁺ nahi). Anode: **I₂** (I⁻, OH⁻ se pehle). Ghol mein K⁺ aur OH⁻: **KOH**. Overall: 2KI + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂ + I₂. Numbers: I₂ = 76.2 ÷ 254 = **0.3 mol**. 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻: electrons = 0.3 × 2 = **0.6 mol**. H₂ = 0.3 mol = 0.3 × 2 = **0.6 g**. KOH = 2 × 0.3 = 0.6 mol; KOH = 39 + 16 + 1 = 56 g/mol, to 0.6 × 56 = **33.6 g**. Jaanch (mass conservation): KI 0.6 mol (0.6 × 166 = 99.6 g) + H₂O 0.6 mol (10.8 g) = 110.4 g; KOH 33.6 + H₂ 0.6 + I₂ 76.2 = 110.4 g ✅.\n5. Cu = 3.81 ÷ 63.5 = **0.06 mol**. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu: electrons = 0.06 × 2 = **0.12 mol**. Anode: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻: 0.12 mol electrons par 0.06 mol Cl₂ = 0.06 × 71 = **4.26 g**. Jaanch: 0.06 mol CuCl₂ = 0.06 × 134.5 = 8.07 g = 3.81 + 4.26 ✅. Chlorine zehreeli hai: isliye sirf padhne ke liye; copper sulphate/chloride ghol bhi nuksandeh hai.\n6. Anode **copper hai (active)**: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ (anode ghulta hai, koi O₂ nahi). Shuru mein ghol mein Cu²⁺ nahi hai, isliye cathode (platinum) par H⁺ discharge hota hai: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Ghol dheere-dheere **neela** hota jaata hai kyunki Cu²⁺ ghol mein aata rehta hai. (Aage chalkar Cu²⁺ bhi cathode par jama hone lagta hai, kyunki order mein wo H⁺ se upar hai.)\n7. Ions: Na⁺, SO₄²⁻, H⁺, OH⁻. Cathode **H₂**, anode **O₂**. 0.5 mol electrons: H₂ = 0.5 ÷ 2 = 0.25 mol = **0.5 g**; O₂ = 0.5 ÷ 4 = 0.125 mol = 0.125 × 32 = **4 g**. Paani kam hota hai (2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂), Na₂SO₄ nahi, isliye Na₂SO₄ ka concentration **thoda badhta hai**. Jaanch: paani ghata = 0.25 mol × 18 = 4.5 g; H₂ + O₂ = 0.5 + 4 = 4.5 g ✅.\n8. Cathode par pehle **Ag** (sabse aasaan), jab Ag⁺ khatam ho gaya to **Cu**, jab Cu²⁺ bhi khatam ho gaya to **H₂** (H⁺ Zn²⁺ se aasaan, isliye zinc kabhi nahi jamta). Anode par hamesha **O₂** (NO₃⁻ nahi, OH⁻ discharge). Order: Ag, phir Cu, phir H₂.\n9. Ions: Na⁺, Br⁻, H⁺, OH⁻. Cathode: **H₂**. Anode: **Br₂** (Br⁻, OH⁻ se pehle): 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻. Ghol mein **NaOH**. Br⁻ aur Cl⁻ ke mixture mein **Br⁻ pehle** discharge hota hai (Br⁻ order mein Cl⁻ se upar). Bromine zehreeli, laal-bhura vapour: sirf padhne ke liye.\n10. Bahut dilute NaCl mein Cl⁻ bahut kam hai, aur paani ka OH⁻ ab bheed mein jeet jaata hai: **O₂**. Concentrated mein Cl⁻ bahut hain aur order mein bhi pehle, isliye **Cl₂**. Aqueous NaCl mein cathode par H⁺ aur Na⁺ dono hain, H⁺ aasaan hai, to H₂; sodium kabhi nahi. Sodium **pighle NaCl** ke electrolysis se milta hai (Chapter 3), jahan paani aur uske H⁺ hi nahi hote.\n11. (a) **False** ❌: cathode par H₂ banta hai, sodium nahi. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **False** ❌: rang waisa ka waisa rehta hai, kyunki jitna Cu²⁺ cathode par gaya utna anode se aaya. (d) **False** ❌: SO₄²⁻ nahi, OH⁻ discharge hota hai, O₂ milti hai. (e) **False** ❌: electrons **tar** mein chalte hain (anode se battery ke raaste cathode tak); ghol mein **ions** bijli le jaate hain.\n12. Order (aasaan pehle): **Ag⁺, H⁺, Ni²⁺, Zn²⁺, Na⁺**. Dilute paani wale ghol mein **Ni²⁺, Zn²⁺ aur Na⁺** ki jagah H⁺ discharge hota hai (H₂ banta hai), kyunki H⁺ order mein unse upar hai; Ag⁺ aur H⁺ discharge hote hain. **Na⁺** kisi bhi paani wale ghol se kabhi nahi; Ni²⁺ apne gaadhe ghol se plating mein jam sakta hai (Part A ki chhoot, Topic 4).\n\n---\n\nNishka ne notebook ke aakhir mein ek chhoti table banayi: electrolyte, electrode, cathode, anode, ghol. \"Chacha, ab main kisi bhi ghol ko dekh kar pehle se bata sakti hoon.\" Mr. Sathaye ne pump-room ka darwaza band kiya: \"Aur ab ek sawaal: agar hum **anode taambe ka** rakhein, aur **cathode** par ek **lohe ki chaabi**, to kya hoga?\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 4 mein hum isi taambe ke anode wale cell ko **electroplating** mein badalte hain: chaabi ya thali par taamba, nickel ya chaandi ki patli parat chadhana. Kaise? Kaun anode, kaun cathode, kaun sa ghol? Wahan dekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Har sawaal mein electrolyte, uski concentration aur electrodes diye gaye hain. Saare electrolysis **sirf padhne ke liye ya teacher demo** hain.\n\n1. Dilute calcium nitrate, Ca(NO₃)₂, solution is electrolysed using inert platinum electrodes. Name the ions present, the product at each electrode, and write the half-equations. State the volume ratio of the two gases.\n2. Concentrated hydrochloric acid is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Name the ions present and the product at each electrode, with half-equations. (Chlorine is poisonous: described only.)\n3. Dilute silver nitrate solution is electrolysed using inert platinum electrodes. Give the product at each electrode, write the overall equation, and say how the solution changes. Then predict what changes if the platinum is replaced by silver electrodes.\n4. Concentrated potassium iodide solution is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Give the product at each electrode, the substance left in the solution, and the overall equation. If 76.2 g of iodine is formed, find the moles of electrons used, the mass of hydrogen and the mass of KOH formed. (I = 127.)\n5. Concentrated copper(II) chloride solution is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. If 3.81 g of copper is deposited, find the moles of electrons, and the mass of chlorine formed. Why is this a described-only demonstration?\n6. Dilute sulphuric acid is electrolysed with a copper anode and a platinum cathode. Give the product at each electrode in the first few minutes, and say what colour change is seen in the solution.\n7. Dilute sodium sulphate solution is electrolysed using inert platinum electrodes. Give the products. If 0.5 mol of electrons pass, find the mass of each gas, and say how the concentration of Na₂SO₄ changes.\n8. A dilute solution contains silver nitrate, copper(II) nitrate and zinc nitrate. It is electrolysed with platinum electrodes for a long time. It is known that Ag⁺ is discharged more easily than Cu²⁺, Cu²⁺ more easily than H⁺, and H⁺ more easily than Zn²⁺, and that NO₃⁻ is not discharged. Give the order of the products at the cathode, and the product at the anode.\n9. Concentrated sodium bromide solution is electrolysed using graphite electrodes. Name the products at both electrodes and the substance left in solution. Which is discharged first from a concentrated mixture of NaBr and NaCl at the anode? (Bromine is poisonous: described only.)\n10. Explain why very dilute NaCl solution gives oxygen at the anode while concentrated NaCl gives chlorine. Also explain why sodium metal can never be obtained by electrolysing an aqueous NaCl solution, and name the method used in Chapter 3.\n11. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) Concentrated NaCl solution gives sodium at the cathode. (b) The anode is the positive electrode in an electrolytic cell. (c) With copper electrodes in CuSO₄ solution the blue colour fades. (d) In dilute H₂SO₄ (platinum electrodes), SO₄²⁻ is discharged at the anode. (e) Electrons travel through the solution from the cathode to the anode.\n12. As a paper task, arrange these cations in the order of discharge, easiest first: Zn²⁺, Ag⁺, Na⁺, H⁺, Ni²⁺. Then say which of them give way to H⁺ in a dilute aqueous solution, and which one can never be obtained from any aqueous solution.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Ions: Ca²⁺, NO₃⁻ (Ca(NO₃)₂ se) aur H⁺, OH⁻ (paani se). Cathode par **H₂** (H⁺ Ca²⁺ se aasaan): 4H⁺ + 4e⁻ → 2H₂. Anode par **O₂** (NO₃⁻ discharge nahi hota): 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻. Volume ratio H₂ : O₂ = **2 : 1**. Overall 2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂ (Ca(NO₃)₂ sirf ions deta hai).\n2. Ions: H⁺, Cl⁻ (HCl se) aur H⁺, OH⁻ (paani se). Cathode: **H₂**: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Anode: ghol **concentrated** hai aur Cl⁻ order mein OH⁻ se pehle, isliye **Cl₂**: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻. Ghol dheere-dheere HCl se khaali hota jaata hai. Chlorine zehreeli hai, isliye sirf fume cupboard mein teacher demo.\n3. Cathode par **Ag**: Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag. Anode par **O₂**: 4OH⁻ → 2H₂O + O₂ + 4e⁻ (NO₃⁻ discharge nahi hota). Overall: 4AgNO₃ + 2H₂O → 4Ag + 4HNO₃ + O₂. Ghol mein **HNO₃** banta hai, ghol **amlin** hota hai aur Ag⁺ kam hota hai. **Silver electrodes** ke saath: anode par silver ghulta hai (Ag → Ag⁺ + e⁻), koi O₂ nahi; cathode par Ag jamta hai; ghol ka concentration waisa ka waisa. Silver nitrate dag chhodta hai aur nuksandeh hai: sirf teacher ke saath.\n4. Ions: K⁺, I⁻, H⁺, OH⁻. Cathode: **H₂** (K⁺ nahi). Anode: **I₂** (I⁻, OH⁻ se pehle). Ghol mein K⁺ aur OH⁻: **KOH**. Overall: 2KI + 2H₂O → 2KOH + H₂ + I₂. Numbers: I₂ = 76.2 ÷ 254 = **0.3 mol**. 2I⁻ → I₂ + 2e⁻: electrons = 0.3 × 2 = **0.6 mol**. H₂ = 0.3 mol = 0.3 × 2 = **0.6 g**. KOH = 2 × 0.3 = 0.6 mol; KOH = 39 + 16 + 1 = 56 g/mol, to 0.6 × 56 = **33.6 g**. Jaanch (mass conservation): KI 0.6 mol (0.6 × 166 = 99.6 g) + H₂O 0.6 mol (10.8 g) = 110.4 g; KOH 33.6 + H₂ 0.6 + I₂ 76.2 = 110.4 g ✅.\n5. Cu = 3.81 ÷ 63.5 = **0.06 mol**. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu: electrons = 0.06 × 2 = **0.12 mol**. Anode: 2Cl⁻ → Cl₂ + 2e⁻: 0.12 mol electrons par 0.06 mol Cl₂ = 0.06 × 71 = **4.26 g**. Jaanch: 0.06 mol CuCl₂ = 0.06 × 134.5 = 8.07 g = 3.81 + 4.26 ✅. Chlorine zehreeli hai: isliye sirf padhne ke liye; copper sulphate/chloride ghol bhi nuksandeh hai.\n6. Anode **copper hai (active)**: Cu → Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ (anode ghulta hai, koi O₂ nahi). Shuru mein ghol mein Cu²⁺ nahi hai, isliye cathode (platinum) par H⁺ discharge hota hai: 2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂. Ghol dheere-dheere **neela** hota jaata hai kyunki Cu²⁺ ghol mein aata rehta hai. (Aage chalkar Cu²⁺ bhi cathode par jama hone lagta hai, kyunki order mein wo H⁺ se upar hai.)\n7. Ions: Na⁺, SO₄²⁻, H⁺, OH⁻. Cathode **H₂**, anode **O₂**. 0.5 mol electrons: H₂ = 0.5 ÷ 2 = 0.25 mol = **0.5 g**; O₂ = 0.5 ÷ 4 = 0.125 mol = 0.125 × 32 = **4 g**. Paani kam hota hai (2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂), Na₂SO₄ nahi, isliye Na₂SO₄ ka concentration **thoda badhta hai**. Jaanch: paani ghata = 0.25 mol × 18 = 4.5 g; H₂ + O₂ = 0.5 + 4 = 4.5 g ✅.\n8. Cathode par pehle **Ag** (sabse aasaan), jab Ag⁺ khatam ho gaya to **Cu**, jab Cu²⁺ bhi khatam ho gaya to **H₂** (H⁺ Zn²⁺ se aasaan, isliye zinc kabhi nahi jamta). Anode par hamesha **O₂** (NO₃⁻ nahi, OH⁻ discharge). Order: Ag, phir Cu, phir H₂.\n9. Ions: Na⁺, Br⁻, H⁺, OH⁻. Cathode: **H₂**. Anode: **Br₂** (Br⁻, OH⁻ se pehle): 2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻. Ghol mein **NaOH**. Br⁻ aur Cl⁻ ke mixture mein **Br⁻ pehle** discharge hota hai (Br⁻ order mein Cl⁻ se upar). Bromine zehreeli, laal-bhura vapour: sirf padhne ke liye.\n10. Bahut dilute NaCl mein Cl⁻ bahut kam hai, aur paani ka OH⁻ ab bheed mein jeet jaata hai: **O₂**. Concentrated mein Cl⁻ bahut hain aur order mein bhi pehle, isliye **Cl₂**. Aqueous NaCl mein cathode par H⁺ aur Na⁺ dono hain, H⁺ aasaan hai, to H₂; sodium kabhi nahi. Sodium **pighle NaCl** ke electrolysis se milta hai (Chapter 3), jahan paani aur uske H⁺ hi nahi hote.\n11. (a) **False** ❌: cathode par H₂ banta hai, sodium nahi. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **False** ❌: rang waisa ka waisa rehta hai, kyunki jitna Cu²⁺ cathode par gaya utna anode se aaya. (d) **False** ❌: SO₄²⁻ nahi, OH⁻ discharge hota hai, O₂ milti hai. (e) **False** ❌: electrons **tar** mein chalte hain (anode se battery ke raaste cathode tak); ghol mein **ions** bijli le jaate hain.\n12. Order (aasaan pehle): **Ag⁺, H⁺, Ni²⁺, Zn²⁺, Na⁺**. Dilute paani wale ghol mein **Ni²⁺, Zn²⁺ aur Na⁺** ki jagah H⁺ discharge hota hai (H₂ banta hai), kyunki H⁺ order mein unse upar hai; Ag⁺ aur H⁺ discharge hote hain. **Na⁺** kisi bhi paani wale ghol se kabhi nahi; Ni²⁺ apne gaadhe ghol se plating mein jam sakta hai (Part A ki chhoot, Topic 4).\n\n---\n\nNishka ne notebook ke aakhir mein ek chhoti table banayi: electrolyte, electrode, cathode, anode, ghol. \"Chacha, ab main kisi bhi ghol ko dekh kar pehle se bata sakti hoon.\" Mr. Sathaye ne pump-room ka darwaza band kiya: \"Aur ab ek sawaal: agar hum **anode taambe ka** rakhein, aur **cathode** par ek **lohe ki chaabi**, to kya hoga?\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Topic 4 mein hum isi taambe ke anode wale cell ko **electroplating** mein badalte hain: chaabi ya thali par taamba, nickel ya chaandi ki patli parat chadhana. Kaise? Kaun anode, kaun cathode, kaun sa ghol? Wahan dekhenge."
          }
