            "correctIndex": 3,
            "explanation": "Pitfall to avoid (\"Electron affinity aur ionisation energy ek hi cheez hai.\"): Dono mein electron ka lena-dena hai, isliye ye dhokha deta hai. Par dono ulte kaam hain. Cl ko hi lo: - Electron affinity: Cl + e⁻ → Cl⁻ + energy. Lagbhag 349 kJ per mol energy nikalti hai. - Ionisation energy: Cl → Cl⁺ + e⁻. Lagbhag 1251 kJ per mol energy lagani padti hai. Ek hi atom, dono process alag, aur dono ki sankhya bhi bahut alag (349 aur 1251). Ek mein electron andar aata hai (energy nikalti hai), doosre mein electron bahar jaata hai (energy lagti hai). Ek aur dekho: Na ki ionisation energy lagbhag 496 hai (Topic 2), par Na ki EA sirf lagbhag 53 hai (chhoti, kyunki metal electron lena nahi chahta). Dono ka mix-up kiya to jawab ulta ho jaayega. Ek chhoti kahani: Sayali ne apni copy mein likha: \"Cl ki EA = 1251.\" Mr. Ubale ne poocha: \"Kya Cl electron lene par 1251 kJ per mol chhodta hai?\" Sayali ne table dekhi: lene par 349 kJ per mol. 1251 to electron nikaalne par lagti hai (IE). Sayali ne kaat kar sahi likha: EA = 349, IE = 1251. Ek aur tareeke se dekho. Cricket mein \"ball pakadna\" aur \"ball fenkna\" bilkul alag kaam hain: pehle mein haath mein ball aati hai, doosre mein haath se jaati hai. Ninad ke liye catch pakadna aasan (EA badi), par wo apni batting mein wicket se door khada rehta hai, matlab ek kaam ka josh doosre kaam ke josh se kuch nahi kehta. Isi tarah ek atom ki EA aur IE ek-doosre se alag sankhyaein hain.",
