          {
            "heading": "Part E: Kisi bhi formula mein bond ginne ka tareeka",
            "headingHinglish": "Part E: Kisi bhi formula mein bond ginne ka tareeka",
            "body": "Ab hum seekhe hue ko ek **chaar-kadam ke niyam** mein likh lete hain.\n\n1. **Ions dhundo.** Formula ko charge se todo. Metal ya NH₄ aam taur par positive ion banata hai, aur Cl, OH jaise hisse negative. Kul charge 0 hona chahiye.\n2. **Har ion ke andar dekho.** Agar ion ek hi atom ka hai (Na⁺, Cl⁻) to andar koi bond nahi. Agar jhund hai (OH⁻, NH₄⁺) to andar covalent bond ginte hain: har dash = ek bond.\n3. **Coordinate bond dhundo.** Kya kisi atom ne dono electron diye (lone pair donor) aur kisi ke paas khaali jagah thi (acceptor, jaise H⁺)? NH₄⁺ mein 1 aur H₃O⁺ mein 1 mil gaya.\n4. **Ginti likho:** ionic kitne, saadhe covalent kitne, coordinate kitne.\n\n```\n   Formula --->  ions  --->  ion ke andar covalent  --->  coordinate?  --->  ginti\n   NaOH         Na⁺ OH⁻       1 (O-H)                    koi nahi             ionic 1, cov 1, coord 0\n   NH₄Cl        NH₄⁺ Cl⁻     4 (N-H)                    1 (N → H)            ionic 1, cov 3, coord 1\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.5** – Chaar-kadam wali ginti: har formula ke liye ions, ion ke andar ke covalent, coordinate bond aur phir ginti. Do examples saath mein.\n\n### Bracket wale formula\n\nCa(OH)₂ ka matlab: **1 Ca** aur **2 OH jhund** (bracket ke andar ka poora jhund 2 baar). Atom ginti: Ca 1, O 2, H 2. Charge: Ca²⁺ aur do OH⁻: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. Isliye bracket zaroori hai: Ca(OH)₂ aur CaOH₂ alag cheezein hain (CaOH₂ ka matlab sirf 1 O aur 2 H, jo OH jhund wala nahi). Is par practice mein dekhenge.",
            "bodyHinglish": "Ab hum seekhe hue ko ek **chaar-kadam ke niyam** mein likh lete hain.\n\n1. **Ions dhundo.** Formula ko charge se todo. Metal ya NH₄ aam taur par positive ion banata hai, aur Cl, OH jaise hisse negative. Kul charge 0 hona chahiye.\n2. **Har ion ke andar dekho.** Agar ion ek hi atom ka hai (Na⁺, Cl⁻) to andar koi bond nahi. Agar jhund hai (OH⁻, NH₄⁺) to andar covalent bond ginte hain: har dash = ek bond.\n3. **Coordinate bond dhundo.** Kya kisi atom ne dono electron diye (lone pair donor) aur kisi ke paas khaali jagah thi (acceptor, jaise H⁺)? NH₄⁺ mein 1 aur H₃O⁺ mein 1 mil gaya.\n4. **Ginti likho:** ionic kitne, saadhe covalent kitne, coordinate kitne.\n\n```\n   Formula --->  ions  --->  ion ke andar covalent  --->  coordinate?  --->  ginti\n   NaOH         Na⁺ OH⁻       1 (O-H)                    koi nahi             ionic 1, cov 1, coord 0\n   NH₄Cl        NH₄⁺ Cl⁻     4 (N-H)                    1 (N → H)            ionic 1, cov 3, coord 1\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.5** – Chaar-kadam wali ginti: har formula ke liye ions, ion ke andar ke covalent, coordinate bond aur phir ginti. Do examples saath mein.\n\n### Bracket wale formula\n\nCa(OH)₂ ka matlab: **1 Ca** aur **2 OH jhund** (bracket ke andar ka poora jhund 2 baar). Atom ginti: Ca 1, O 2, H 2. Charge: Ca²⁺ aur do OH⁻: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅. Isliye bracket zaroori hai: Ca(OH)₂ aur CaOH₂ alag cheezein hain (CaOH₂ ka matlab sirf 1 O aur 2 H, jo OH jhund wala nahi). Is par practice mein dekhenge."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Part F: Ye compounds ionic salt jaise kyun behave karte hain?",
            "headingHinglish": "Part F: Ye compounds ionic salt jaise kyun behave karte hain?",
            "body": "NaOH aur NH₄Cl dono **ions ke crystal** (lattice) se bane hain: Na⁺ aur OH⁻ ya NH₄⁺ aur Cl⁻ ek regular jaali mein. Isliye ye **ionic compounds** ke gun dikhate hain (Class 9: thos, kadak, ghol mein bijli). NaOH ek kadak safed thos hai jise pighlane ke liye kaafi garmi chahiye. (NH₄Cl garam karne par pighalta nahi, balki toot kar NH₃ aur HCl gas ban kar udta hai aur thande hisse par phir jud jaata hai: ye sirf padhne ke liye, ghar par kabhi garam nahi karna.) Sab bond ki tulna aur melting ke numbers Topic 5 mein ek table mein.\n\nPar ek bahut zaroori baat: **paani mein ghulne par kya tootta hai?**\n\n```\n   NaOH  (paani mein)  --->   Na⁺   +   OH⁻\n   NH₄Cl (paani mein)  --->   NH₄⁺  +   Cl⁻\n\n   Ionic bond TOOTA (ions alag ho gaye).\n   Ion ke ANDAR ke covalent bond (O-H, N-H) NAHI TOOTE:\n   OH⁻ aur NH₄⁺ poore jhund ki tarah ghol mein ghoomte hain.\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.6** – Paani mein ionic bond toot-ta hai, ion ke andar ke covalent bond (O–H, N–H aur coordinate N → H) nahi. Isliye ghol mein Na⁺, OH⁻, NH₄⁺, Cl⁻ jaise **poore ions** milte hain.\n\nIsi wajah se Chapter 4 (electrolysis) mein NaOH ko **strong electrolyte** kaha tha: lagbhag poora Na⁺ aur OH⁻ mein tootta hai. NH₄Cl bhi ek salt hai aur paani mein ions deta hai. NH₄OH (ammonium hydroxide) ko wahan **weak electrolyte** kaha tha, kyunki wo kam toot-ta hai, aur uske ions NH₄⁺ aur OH⁻ hain.",
            "bodyHinglish": "NaOH aur NH₄Cl dono **ions ke crystal** (lattice) se bane hain: Na⁺ aur OH⁻ ya NH₄⁺ aur Cl⁻ ek regular jaali mein. Isliye ye **ionic compounds** ke gun dikhate hain (Class 9: thos, kadak, ghol mein bijli). NaOH ek kadak safed thos hai jise pighlane ke liye kaafi garmi chahiye. (NH₄Cl garam karne par pighalta nahi, balki toot kar NH₃ aur HCl gas ban kar udta hai aur thande hisse par phir jud jaata hai: ye sirf padhne ke liye, ghar par kabhi garam nahi karna.) Sab bond ki tulna aur melting ke numbers Topic 5 mein ek table mein.\n\nPar ek bahut zaroori baat: **paani mein ghulne par kya tootta hai?**\n\n```\n   NaOH  (paani mein)  --->   Na⁺   +   OH⁻\n   NH₄Cl (paani mein)  --->   NH₄⁺  +   Cl⁻\n\n   Ionic bond TOOTA (ions alag ho gaye).\n   Ion ke ANDAR ke covalent bond (O-H, N-H) NAHI TOOTE:\n   OH⁻ aur NH₄⁺ poore jhund ki tarah ghol mein ghoomte hain.\n```\n\n> **Figure 4.6** – Paani mein ionic bond toot-ta hai, ion ke andar ke covalent bond (O–H, N–H aur coordinate N → H) nahi. Isliye ghol mein Na⁺, OH⁻, NH₄⁺, Cl⁻ jaise **poore ions** milte hain.\n\nIsi wajah se Chapter 4 (electrolysis) mein NaOH ko **strong electrolyte** kaha tha: lagbhag poora Na⁺ aur OH⁻ mein tootta hai. NH₄Cl bhi ek salt hai aur paani mein ions deta hai. NH₄OH (ammonium hydroxide) ko wahan **weak electrolyte** kaha tha, kyunki wo kam toot-ta hai, aur uske ions NH₄⁺ aur OH⁻ hain."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Khud karke dekho: block se model",
            "headingHinglish": "Khud karke dekho: block se model",
            "body": "(Ghar par adult ke saath. Koi chemical nahi, sirf building blocks ya Lego.)\n\nDo alag rang ke blocks lo: **laal** (O) aur **safed** (H). Unhe pakke jodo: ye ek **OH jhund** hai. Uspar ek chhoti chit lagao \"−1\". Ek **peela** block lo (Na) aur uspar chit \"+1\". Ab peele block ko OH jhund ke paas rakh do (halke se, ya ek chhote magnet se jodo): ye hai **NaOH**. Dhyan do: laal-safed ke beech ka jod pakka, peele aur laal-safed ke beech ka jod halka.\n\nAb NH₄Cl ke liye ek **hare** block (N) ke chaaron taraf 4 **safed** blocks jodo, aur ek safed ko **ek alag rang ke dhaage** (tir) se jodo ye dikhane ke liye ki wo coordinate hai. Poore jhund par chit \"+1\". Ek **neela** block (Cl) par \"−1\" lagao aur use halke se paas rakho. Ginti karo: kitne pakke jod, kitne tir, kitne halke jod? (Jawab: 3 pakke, 1 tir, 1 halka.)",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Ghar par adult ke saath. Koi chemical nahi, sirf building blocks ya Lego.)\n\nDo alag rang ke blocks lo: **laal** (O) aur **safed** (H). Unhe pakke jodo: ye ek **OH jhund** hai. Uspar ek chhoti chit lagao \"−1\". Ek **peela** block lo (Na) aur uspar chit \"+1\". Ab peele block ko OH jhund ke paas rakh do (halke se, ya ek chhote magnet se jodo): ye hai **NaOH**. Dhyan do: laal-safed ke beech ka jod pakka, peele aur laal-safed ke beech ka jod halka.\n\nAb NH₄Cl ke liye ek **hare** block (N) ke chaaron taraf 4 **safed** blocks jodo, aur ek safed ko **ek alag rang ke dhaage** (tir) se jodo ye dikhane ke liye ki wo coordinate hai. Poore jhund par chit \"+1\". Ek **neela** block (Cl) par \"−1\" lagao aur use halke se paas rakho. Ginti karo: kitne pakke jod, kitne tir, kitne halke jod? (Jawab: 3 pakke, 1 tir, 1 halka.)"
          },
          {
            "heading": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "headingHinglish": "Aam galtiyan (bachke rehna)",
            "body": "**Galti: \"Jis compound mein metal hai, wo purely ionic hai, aur kahin koi covalent bond nahi.\"**\nYe sach lagta hai kyunki NaCl, MgO aur Na₂O jaise Class 9 ke examples mein metal ke saath sirf ionic bond tha. Par ye **hamesha sahi nahi**. Demo teen kadam mein:\n\n1. **NaOH ko lo.** Isme metal hai (Na). Ab ions todo: Na⁺ aur OH⁻. OH⁻ ke andar O aur H ke beech **share kiya hua electron-jodi** hai: ye covalent bond hai (Part B). To NaOH mein **1 ionic aur 1 covalent** bond hai. Purely ionic nahi.\n2. **Na₂O se tulna karo** (Class 9 se). Na₂O mein 2 Na⁺ aur 1 O²⁻ hain. Dono ek-atom ke ion hain, to **andar koi covalent bond nahi**: ye sach mein **sirf ionic** hai. Fark kahan? Na₂O ke negative ion O²⁻ ek **akela atom** hai, jabki NaOH ke negative ion OH⁻ **atoms ka jhund** hai.\n3. **Niyam:** metal wala compound sirf tab purely ionic hai jab uska negative ion bhi ek-atom ka ho (Cl⁻, O²⁻, S²⁻). Agar negative ya positive ion polyatomic hai (OH⁻, NH₄⁺), to uske **andar covalent bond** hote hain, aur poore compound mein **dono kisam** ke bond hote hain.\n\nEk chhota test: formula mein bracket ya jhund wala ion dikhe (jaise Ca(OH)₂, NaOH) to socho \"andar covalent bond hai kya?\" Haan hi hoga.",
            "bodyHinglish": "**Galti: \"Jis compound mein metal hai, wo purely ionic hai, aur kahin koi covalent bond nahi.\"**\nYe sach lagta hai kyunki NaCl, MgO aur Na₂O jaise Class 9 ke examples mein metal ke saath sirf ionic bond tha. Par ye **hamesha sahi nahi**. Demo teen kadam mein:\n\n1. **NaOH ko lo.** Isme metal hai (Na). Ab ions todo: Na⁺ aur OH⁻. OH⁻ ke andar O aur H ke beech **share kiya hua electron-jodi** hai: ye covalent bond hai (Part B). To NaOH mein **1 ionic aur 1 covalent** bond hai. Purely ionic nahi.\n2. **Na₂O se tulna karo** (Class 9 se). Na₂O mein 2 Na⁺ aur 1 O²⁻ hain. Dono ek-atom ke ion hain, to **andar koi covalent bond nahi**: ye sach mein **sirf ionic** hai. Fark kahan? Na₂O ke negative ion O²⁻ ek **akela atom** hai, jabki NaOH ke negative ion OH⁻ **atoms ka jhund** hai.\n3. **Niyam:** metal wala compound sirf tab purely ionic hai jab uska negative ion bhi ek-atom ka ho (Cl⁻, O²⁻, S²⁻). Agar negative ya positive ion polyatomic hai (OH⁻, NH₄⁺), to uske **andar covalent bond** hote hain, aur poore compound mein **dono kisam** ke bond hote hain.\n\nEk chhota test: formula mein bracket ya jhund wala ion dikhe (jaise Ca(OH)₂, NaOH) to socho \"andar covalent bond hai kya?\" Haan hi hoga."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Quick Recap",
            "headingHinglish": "Quick Recap",
            "body": "- **Polyatomic ion** = atoms ka jhund (andar covalent bond) jis par ek kul charge hai, aur jo ek ion ki tarah behave karta hai: OH⁻, NH₄⁺.\n- **OH⁻**: O ke apne 6, H ka 1, aur ek extra electron = 8 electron. O par 3 lone pairs aur 1 shared pair (O–H). O–H ka farq 3.5 − 2.1 = 1.4, polar covalent.\n- **NaOH** = Na⁺ + OH⁻: **1 ionic + 1 covalent**, coordinate 0. Na–O ka farq 2.6 (bahut bada), O–H ka 1.4.\n- **NH₄Cl** = NH₄⁺ + Cl⁻: **1 ionic + 3 saadhe covalent (N–H) + 1 coordinate (N → H)**: teeno kisam.\n- **Chaar-kadam ginti:** ions dhundo, andar ke covalent ginno, coordinate dhundo, ginti likho.\n- Bracket wale formula mein ion jhund ek se zyada baar: Ca(OH)₂ mein 1 Ca aur 2 OH.\n- Paani mein **sirf ionic bond** tootta hai, ion ke andar ke covalent bond nahi.\n- \"Metal wala compound purely ionic\" hamesha sahi nahi: NaOH mein covalent bhi hai. Na₂O purely ionic hai.",
            "bodyHinglish": "- **Polyatomic ion** = atoms ka jhund (andar covalent bond) jis par ek kul charge hai, aur jo ek ion ki tarah behave karta hai: OH⁻, NH₄⁺.\n- **OH⁻**: O ke apne 6, H ka 1, aur ek extra electron = 8 electron. O par 3 lone pairs aur 1 shared pair (O–H). O–H ka farq 3.5 − 2.1 = 1.4, polar covalent.\n- **NaOH** = Na⁺ + OH⁻: **1 ionic + 1 covalent**, coordinate 0. Na–O ka farq 2.6 (bahut bada), O–H ka 1.4.\n- **NH₄Cl** = NH₄⁺ + Cl⁻: **1 ionic + 3 saadhe covalent (N–H) + 1 coordinate (N → H)**: teeno kisam.\n- **Chaar-kadam ginti:** ions dhundo, andar ke covalent ginno, coordinate dhundo, ginti likho.\n- Bracket wale formula mein ion jhund ek se zyada baar: Ca(OH)₂ mein 1 Ca aur 2 OH.\n- Paani mein **sirf ionic bond** tootta hai, ion ke andar ke covalent bond nahi.\n- \"Metal wala compound purely ionic\" hamesha sahi nahi: NaOH mein covalent bhi hai. Na₂O purely ionic hai."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain.)\n\nUse these approximate values where needed: H 2.1, N 3.0, O 3.5, K 0.8, Ca 1.0.\n\n1. Potassium hydroxide, KOH, is made of K⁺ and OH⁻. List the bonds in one formula unit (ionic, covalent, coordinate) with a count of each.\n2. Draw the dot-and-cross diagram of the OH⁻ part of KOH. Count the electrons around O and around H. Which atom supplied the extra electron, and how many electrons does K have in its outer shell after it gives one away (K: 2, 8, 8, 1)?\n3. Ca(OH)₂ contains one Ca²⁺ and two OH⁻. Show the charge balance. Count the ionic attractions and O–H covalent bonds in one formula unit, and also the atoms of Ca, O and H in the formula.\n4. Ammonium hydroxide, NH₄OH, is made of NH₄⁺ and OH⁻. Count its ionic, ordinary covalent and coordinate bonds. Is the ionic bond one of the bonds you count inside NH₄⁺?\n5. Ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃, contains NH₄⁺ and NO₃⁻. Using the charges, find the charge on NO₃⁻. Without drawing NO₃⁻, say how many ionic bonds per formula unit and how many N–H bonds, including coordinate, are in the NH₄⁺ part.\n6. Find the electronegativity differences for K–O, Ca–O, O–H and N–H. Arrange them in decreasing order, and say which are ionic-like and which are polar covalent.\n7. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) In OH⁻, oxygen has one lone pair. (b) In KOH the potassium ion is joined to oxygen by a shared pair. (c) Ca(OH)₂ has no covalent bonds because Ca is a metal. (d) NH₄⁺ in NH₄NO₃ has 4 N–H bonds in all.\n8. Solid KOH does not conduct electricity, but its water solution does. Explain which bond breaks when KOH dissolves, which bond stays, and why the solution conducts.\n9. A student writes the formula of calcium hydroxide as CaOH₂. Find the atoms in CaOH₂ and in Ca(OH)₂, and explain why the bracket matters.\n10. NH₄OH is called a weak electrolyte in Chapter 4. Write the ions it gives in water, and say which kind of bond is broken and which bonds stay intact inside those ions.\n11. Describe a block-model that shows the bonds of KOH: which blocks are joined firmly and which loosely, with the sign tags.\n12. Why does the O–H bond not break into separate H⁺ and O²⁻ ions when KOH dissolves in water? Use the electronegativity difference.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. KOH → K⁺ + OH⁻, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅. **Ionic: 1** (K⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech), **saadha covalent: 1** (O–H, OH⁻ ke andar), **coordinate: 0**. Jaanch: formula mein K, O, H sab 1-1 ✅.\n2. Dot diagram: O ke 6 dots, H ka x, extra electron o (Figure 4.2 jaisa). O ke paas 3 lone pairs (6 e) + 1 shared pair (2 e) = **8** ✅, H ke paas 2 ✅, kul 6 + 1 + 1 = 8 ✅. Extra electron **K** ne diya. K ke paas ab 2, 8, 8 hain, yaani baahari shell mein **8** electron ✅.\n3. Ca²⁺ + 2 OH⁻: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = **0** ✅. **Ionic attractions: 2** (ek Ca²⁺ ke do OH⁻ partner), **O–H covalent bonds: 2**, coordinate 0. Atoms: Ca 1, O 2, H 2 ✅. Ca ke 2 baahari electron the, har OH⁻ ko 1-1 mila ✅.\n4. NH₄⁺ ke andar 3 saadhe N–H aur 1 coordinate N → H; OH⁻ ke andar 1 O–H covalent; NH₄⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech 1 ionic. **Ionic: 1, saadha covalent: 3 + 1 = 4 (N–H ×3 aur O–H ×1), coordinate: 1.** Nahi: ionic bond NH₄⁺ ke **andar** ke bonds mein shaamil nahi, wo ions ke **beech** ka bond hai. Jaanch: NH₄⁺ mein 4 bond = 4 H ✅.\n5. Charge balance: NH₄⁺ (+1) + NO₃⁻ ka charge = 0, to NO₃⁻ ka charge **−1**. **Ionic bond: 1** per formula unit. NH₄⁺ mein **4 N–H bond**: 3 saadhe + 1 coordinate. Jaanch: 3 + 1 = 4 ✅ (NO₃⁻ ke andar ke bond ko hum yahan gine nahi rahe).\n6. K–O: 3.5 − 0.8 = **2.7**; Ca–O: 3.5 − 1.0 = **2.5**; O–H: 3.5 − 2.1 = **1.4**; N–H: 3.0 − 2.1 = **0.9**. Decreasing: **K–O (2.7) > Ca–O (2.5) > O–H (1.4) > N–H (0.9)**. K–O aur Ca–O ionic-like (bahut bada farq), O–H aur N–H polar covalent. Jaanch: 2.7 > 2.5 > 1.4 > 0.9 ✅.\n7. (a) **False** ❌: O par **3** lone pairs hain (6 e). (b) **False** ❌: K⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech **ionic** bond hai, shared pair nahi. (c) **False** ❌: har OH⁻ ke andar O–H covalent bond hai, to 2 covalent bonds. (d) **True** ✅: 3 saadhe + 1 coordinate = 4.\n8. Solid KOH mein K⁺ aur OH⁻ lattice mein jakde hain, free nahi, to bijli nahi chalti. Paani mein **ionic bond tootta hai**: K⁺ aur OH⁻ free hokar ghol mein ghoomte hain. **O–H covalent bond nahi tootta**: OH⁻ poore jhund ki tarah rehta hai. Free ions charge le jaate hain, isliye ghol bijli chalata hai (Chapter 4).\n9. CaOH₂: Ca 1, O 1, H 2. Ca(OH)₂: Ca 1, O **2**, H 2. Bracket batata hai ki OH jhund **do baar** hai, to O bhi 2. Bina bracket ke ek O hi rehta hai aur charge balance bhi nahi banta (Ca²⁺ ke liye 2 OH⁻ chahiye). Jaanch: bracket wale mein charge +2 + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅.\n10. NH₄OH ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ (⇌ isliye ki thoda hi toota hai, baaki NH₄OH hi rehta hai). Jo bond tootta hai wo NH₄⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech ka **ionic** bond hai. **Bachte hain:** NH₄⁺ ke andar 3 saadhe N–H aur 1 coordinate N → H, aur OH⁻ ke andar 1 O–H covalent. Jaanch: atom ginti N 1, H 5, O 1 dono taraf ✅, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n11. Ek laal block (O) aur ek safed block (H) **pakke** jode jaate hain: ye OH jhund hai, uspar \"−1\" ka chit. Ek peela block (K) par \"+1\" ka chit, jise OH jhund ke paas **halke se** (ya magnet se) jodo. Pakka jod = O–H covalent. Halka jod = K⁺ aur OH⁻ ka ionic bond. Ginti: 1 pakka, 1 halka. Jaanch: formula KOH mein 1 K, 1 O, 1 H ✅.\n12. O–H ka farq 3.5 − 2.1 = **1.4**, jo polar covalent ke beech wale hisse mein hai, ionic ke itna bada nahi. Shared pair O ki taraf zyada khisakta hai par poora O ke paas nahi jaata, to O aur H ke beech bond covalent hi rehta hai. Paani mein sirf K⁺–OH⁻ wala ionic bond tootta hai. Jaanch: 1.4 < 2 ✅.\n\n---\n\nMandar ne block-set se do chhote model banaye aur chit lagakar bola, \"Chacha, ab main kisi bhi compound ko dekhte hi bata sakta hoon ki andar ke pakke jod kahan hain aur bahar ka halka jod kahan.\" Mr. Barve ne hanskar kaha, \"Bas, ab NH₄Cl ke saath apna model mat bhool jaana, usme teen tarah ke jod hain!\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab hum ionic, polar covalent, non-polar covalent aur coordinate, sabhi bond kisam ke beech ka farq ek hi bade table mein rakhenge: kaun pighlega kaun nahi, kaun ghulega kaun nahi, aur ye gun asal mein bond ki banawat se kaise nikalte hain. Topic 5 mein.",
            "bodyHinglish": "(Sab sawaal paper par hal karne ke liye hain.)\n\nUse these approximate values where needed: H 2.1, N 3.0, O 3.5, K 0.8, Ca 1.0.\n\n1. Potassium hydroxide, KOH, is made of K⁺ and OH⁻. List the bonds in one formula unit (ionic, covalent, coordinate) with a count of each.\n2. Draw the dot-and-cross diagram of the OH⁻ part of KOH. Count the electrons around O and around H. Which atom supplied the extra electron, and how many electrons does K have in its outer shell after it gives one away (K: 2, 8, 8, 1)?\n3. Ca(OH)₂ contains one Ca²⁺ and two OH⁻. Show the charge balance. Count the ionic attractions and O–H covalent bonds in one formula unit, and also the atoms of Ca, O and H in the formula.\n4. Ammonium hydroxide, NH₄OH, is made of NH₄⁺ and OH⁻. Count its ionic, ordinary covalent and coordinate bonds. Is the ionic bond one of the bonds you count inside NH₄⁺?\n5. Ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃, contains NH₄⁺ and NO₃⁻. Using the charges, find the charge on NO₃⁻. Without drawing NO₃⁻, say how many ionic bonds per formula unit and how many N–H bonds, including coordinate, are in the NH₄⁺ part.\n6. Find the electronegativity differences for K–O, Ca–O, O–H and N–H. Arrange them in decreasing order, and say which are ionic-like and which are polar covalent.\n7. State True or False and correct the false ones: (a) In OH⁻, oxygen has one lone pair. (b) In KOH the potassium ion is joined to oxygen by a shared pair. (c) Ca(OH)₂ has no covalent bonds because Ca is a metal. (d) NH₄⁺ in NH₄NO₃ has 4 N–H bonds in all.\n8. Solid KOH does not conduct electricity, but its water solution does. Explain which bond breaks when KOH dissolves, which bond stays, and why the solution conducts.\n9. A student writes the formula of calcium hydroxide as CaOH₂. Find the atoms in CaOH₂ and in Ca(OH)₂, and explain why the bracket matters.\n10. NH₄OH is called a weak electrolyte in Chapter 4. Write the ions it gives in water, and say which kind of bond is broken and which bonds stay intact inside those ions.\n11. Describe a block-model that shows the bonds of KOH: which blocks are joined firmly and which loosely, with the sign tags.\n12. Why does the O–H bond not break into separate H⁺ and O²⁻ ions when KOH dissolves in water? Use the electronegativity difference.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. KOH → K⁺ + OH⁻, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅. **Ionic: 1** (K⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech), **saadha covalent: 1** (O–H, OH⁻ ke andar), **coordinate: 0**. Jaanch: formula mein K, O, H sab 1-1 ✅.\n2. Dot diagram: O ke 6 dots, H ka x, extra electron o (Figure 4.2 jaisa). O ke paas 3 lone pairs (6 e) + 1 shared pair (2 e) = **8** ✅, H ke paas 2 ✅, kul 6 + 1 + 1 = 8 ✅. Extra electron **K** ne diya. K ke paas ab 2, 8, 8 hain, yaani baahari shell mein **8** electron ✅.\n3. Ca²⁺ + 2 OH⁻: (+2) + 2 × (−1) = **0** ✅. **Ionic attractions: 2** (ek Ca²⁺ ke do OH⁻ partner), **O–H covalent bonds: 2**, coordinate 0. Atoms: Ca 1, O 2, H 2 ✅. Ca ke 2 baahari electron the, har OH⁻ ko 1-1 mila ✅.\n4. NH₄⁺ ke andar 3 saadhe N–H aur 1 coordinate N → H; OH⁻ ke andar 1 O–H covalent; NH₄⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech 1 ionic. **Ionic: 1, saadha covalent: 3 + 1 = 4 (N–H ×3 aur O–H ×1), coordinate: 1.** Nahi: ionic bond NH₄⁺ ke **andar** ke bonds mein shaamil nahi, wo ions ke **beech** ka bond hai. Jaanch: NH₄⁺ mein 4 bond = 4 H ✅.\n5. Charge balance: NH₄⁺ (+1) + NO₃⁻ ka charge = 0, to NO₃⁻ ka charge **−1**. **Ionic bond: 1** per formula unit. NH₄⁺ mein **4 N–H bond**: 3 saadhe + 1 coordinate. Jaanch: 3 + 1 = 4 ✅ (NO₃⁻ ke andar ke bond ko hum yahan gine nahi rahe).\n6. K–O: 3.5 − 0.8 = **2.7**; Ca–O: 3.5 − 1.0 = **2.5**; O–H: 3.5 − 2.1 = **1.4**; N–H: 3.0 − 2.1 = **0.9**. Decreasing: **K–O (2.7) > Ca–O (2.5) > O–H (1.4) > N–H (0.9)**. K–O aur Ca–O ionic-like (bahut bada farq), O–H aur N–H polar covalent. Jaanch: 2.7 > 2.5 > 1.4 > 0.9 ✅.\n7. (a) **False** ❌: O par **3** lone pairs hain (6 e). (b) **False** ❌: K⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech **ionic** bond hai, shared pair nahi. (c) **False** ❌: har OH⁻ ke andar O–H covalent bond hai, to 2 covalent bonds. (d) **True** ✅: 3 saadhe + 1 coordinate = 4.\n8. Solid KOH mein K⁺ aur OH⁻ lattice mein jakde hain, free nahi, to bijli nahi chalti. Paani mein **ionic bond tootta hai**: K⁺ aur OH⁻ free hokar ghol mein ghoomte hain. **O–H covalent bond nahi tootta**: OH⁻ poore jhund ki tarah rehta hai. Free ions charge le jaate hain, isliye ghol bijli chalata hai (Chapter 4).\n9. CaOH₂: Ca 1, O 1, H 2. Ca(OH)₂: Ca 1, O **2**, H 2. Bracket batata hai ki OH jhund **do baar** hai, to O bhi 2. Bina bracket ke ek O hi rehta hai aur charge balance bhi nahi banta (Ca²⁺ ke liye 2 OH⁻ chahiye). Jaanch: bracket wale mein charge +2 + 2 × (−1) = 0 ✅.\n10. NH₄OH ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻ (⇌ isliye ki thoda hi toota hai, baaki NH₄OH hi rehta hai). Jo bond tootta hai wo NH₄⁺ aur OH⁻ ke beech ka **ionic** bond hai. **Bachte hain:** NH₄⁺ ke andar 3 saadhe N–H aur 1 coordinate N → H, aur OH⁻ ke andar 1 O–H covalent. Jaanch: atom ginti N 1, H 5, O 1 dono taraf ✅, charge (+1) + (−1) = 0 ✅.\n11. Ek laal block (O) aur ek safed block (H) **pakke** jode jaate hain: ye OH jhund hai, uspar \"−1\" ka chit. Ek peela block (K) par \"+1\" ka chit, jise OH jhund ke paas **halke se** (ya magnet se) jodo. Pakka jod = O–H covalent. Halka jod = K⁺ aur OH⁻ ka ionic bond. Ginti: 1 pakka, 1 halka. Jaanch: formula KOH mein 1 K, 1 O, 1 H ✅.\n12. O–H ka farq 3.5 − 2.1 = **1.4**, jo polar covalent ke beech wale hisse mein hai, ionic ke itna bada nahi. Shared pair O ki taraf zyada khisakta hai par poora O ke paas nahi jaata, to O aur H ke beech bond covalent hi rehta hai. Paani mein sirf K⁺–OH⁻ wala ionic bond tootta hai. Jaanch: 1.4 < 2 ✅.\n\n---\n\nMandar ne block-set se do chhote model banaye aur chit lagakar bola, \"Chacha, ab main kisi bhi compound ko dekhte hi bata sakta hoon ki andar ke pakke jod kahan hain aur bahar ka halka jod kahan.\" Mr. Barve ne hanskar kaha, \"Bas, ab NH₄Cl ke saath apna model mat bhool jaana, usme teen tarah ke jod hain!\"\n\n**Aage kya?** Ab hum ionic, polar covalent, non-polar covalent aur coordinate, sabhi bond kisam ke beech ka farq ek hi bade table mein rakhenge: kaun pighlega kaun nahi, kaun ghulega kaun nahi, aur ye gun asal mein bond ki banawat se kaise nikalte hain. Topic 5 mein."
          }
